// Rep Max questions: English Language (AQA 8700), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gel-001","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":1,"q":"AQA Paper 1 Question 1 changed from 2026. What does the student now do?","o":["List four facts in their own words from the opening lines","Write a short paragraph on the writer's word choices","Pick four true statements from a list of eight","Answer four multiple-choice questions about the opening lines"],"a":3,"x":"From 2026, Paper 1 Q1 is four multiple-choice questions (4 marks) on information in the opening lines. Choosing four true statements from eight is Paper 2 Q1.","w":["Describes the old format: listing four facts in your own words, not multiple choice.","Mixes it up with Q2, which is the language paragraph, not a Q1 task.","Confuses it with Paper 2 Q1, where you choose true statements from eight.",""]}
{"id":"gel-002","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":2,"q":"Extract: \"Rain hammered the station roof. Mira clutched her ticket and stared at the empty platform, waiting for a train that was already late.\" What does the extract clearly state?","o":["Mira is frightened of travelling alone","The train Mira is waiting for is late","Mira has already missed her train","Mira is waiting for a friend to arrive"],"a":1,"x":"Q1 tests information stated in the text. The late train is stated directly. Fear is only a guess, she is waiting for a train not a friend, and she has not missed it.","w":["Fear is a guess from her clutching the ticket; the text never says it.","","Misreads \"already late\": the train is late, but she has not missed it.","Assumes a friend; she is waiting for a train, not a person."]}
{"id":"gel-003","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"The old house glared down at the children\"?","o":["Simile","Onomatopoeia","Personification","Rhetorical question"],"a":2,"x":"'Glared' gives the house a human action, so it is personification. There is no 'like' or 'as', no sound word and no question.","w":["No \"like\" or \"as\", so it is not a simile.","No sound word such as \"bang\" or \"hiss\", so not onomatopoeia.","","There is no question in the sentence, so it is not rhetorical."]}
{"id":"gel-004","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":2,"q":"What does Paper 1 Question 2 ask you to do?","o":["Explain how the whole extract is organised to interest the reader","Decide how far you agree with a statement about the extract","Pick out four pieces of information from the opening lines","Explain how the writer's words and phrases create effects in a given part"],"a":3,"x":"Q2 (8 marks) is about language: you comment on chosen words, phrases and techniques and their effects. Structure is Q3 and the statement is Q4.","w":["That is Q3 on structure, not Q2 on language.","That is Q4, where you evaluate a statement.","That is Q1, which tests stated information.",""]}
{"id":"gel-005","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":3,"q":"A student analyses: \"The ice-cold corridor swallowed her footsteps.\" Which comment is strongest?","o":["The writer uses a verb to describe the corridor","This sentence is descriptive and makes the reader want to read on","'Swallowed' makes the corridor seem a hungry creature, so it feels threatening","The corridor is cold and she is walking along it"],"a":2,"x":"Strong analysis picks a precise word, explains what it suggests and links it to an effect. The other comments only name a word class, praise vaguely or retell.","w":["Only names a word class; it does not explain any effect.","Vague praise with no quotation, no word choice and no explanation.","","Just retells the sentence without analysing any word."]}
{"id":"gel-006","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":2,"q":"In \"The clerk scuttled away from the desk\", what does the verb \"scuttled\" suggest?","o":["He walked slowly and calmly across the room","He is proud and wants everyone to notice him","He moves quickly and nervously, like a small frightened creature","The writer is simply naming his job"],"a":2,"x":"'Scuttled' is usually used for insects or small animals running fast, so it suggests quick, nervous, timid movement.","w":["Opposite meaning: \"scuttled\" means fast and hurried, not slow and calm.","Nothing suggests pride; \"scuttled\" implies nervous, not showy.","","Reads it as just labelling him; the verb shows how he moves."]}
{"id":"gel-007","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a structural feature a student could comment on in Paper 1 Q3?","o":["A simile comparing the sun to a coin","The verb \"stormed\" in the third line","The adjective \"dark\" in the opening line","A shift of focus from a wide view of a town to one character's face"],"a":3,"x":"Structure is about how the text is organised: shifts of focus, changes of place or time, openings and endings. Similes and single words are language features.","w":["A simile is a language feature, not structure.","A single verb is word choice, which belongs to language.","A single adjective is word choice, which belongs to language.",""]}
{"id":"gel-008","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":2,"q":"Paper 1 Question 3 asks you to write about the structure of which part of the extract?","o":["The whole extract, from beginning to end","Only the first paragraph","One sentence chosen by the examiner","Only the final line"],"a":0,"x":"Q3 looks at the whole source: how it opens, where the focus shifts and how it ends. Q2 and Q4 look at specific parts.","w":["","Thinks Q3 is only about the opening; it covers the whole text.","Q3 covers the whole extract, not one sentence picked out for you.","Q3 covers the whole extract, not only the ending."]}
{"id":"gel-009","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":2,"q":"A story shows a girl at her bedroom window, then the garden, then the gate, then the empty road beyond. How does the focus move?","o":["From a wide landscape zooming in to one tiny detail","From the past to the present through a flashback","From one narrator's voice to another narrator's voice","Outwards from her room, further and further away"],"a":3,"x":"Window, garden, gate, road: each place is further from the girl, so the focus moves outwards. This is a structural shift of focus.","w":["Reversed: the focus moves out from her room to the road, not in to a detail.","No flashback; every place is in the same moment.","There is only one voice; no second narrator appears.",""]}
{"id":"gel-010","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":3,"q":"An extract opens with a calm description of a village, then sentences get shorter, ending with a one-line paragraph: \"The door was open.\" How does this build tension?","o":["Calm turns to short, uneasy sentences and an ending that leaves a question","The calm opening continues to the end, keeping the reader relaxed","A long final paragraph answers every question the reader has","The writer switches to a new narrator for the last line"],"a":0,"x":"Contrast between calm and uneasy, shortening sentences and a one-line cliffhanger paragraph are structural choices that create suspense.","w":["","The sentences get shorter and the ending is uneasy, so the calm does not last.","The ending is one short line that raises a question, not a long answer.","No new narrator appears; the tension comes from sentence length and the ending."]}
{"id":"gel-011","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":1,"q":"How many marks is Paper 1 Question 4 worth?","o":["20","8","4","40"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 is the biggest reading question: 20 marks for evaluating a statement. Q2 and Q3 are 8 each, Q1 is 4 and Q5 writing is 40.","w":["","8 marks is Q2 or Q3, not Q4.","4 marks is Q1.","40 marks is the Q5 writing task."]}
{"id":"gel-012","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":2,"q":"In Q4 a student writes only \"I agree\" and retells the story. What is missing?","o":["A list of every technique in the whole extract","A paragraph about the writer's life and times","A judgement on the statement, with evidence and comments on methods","A summary of the answers to Q2 and Q3"],"a":2,"x":"Q4 rewards a judgement on how far you agree and/or disagree, backed by short quotations and comments on the writer's methods. Retelling earns little.","w":["Listing every technique is not needed; Q4 wants a judgement on the statement.","The writer's life and times are not assessed in Q4.","","Q4 does not summarise earlier answers; it needs fresh evidence and a judgement."]}
{"id":"gel-013","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":3,"q":"Passage: \"Tom folded his arms and smiled. Then the phone rang. The smile slid from his face.\" Statement: \"In this passage, Tom is calm.\" Which response is best?","o":["Partly: he seems calm at first, but 'slid' suggests his calm draining away","I agree, because Tom smiles, which shows he is calm","I disagree, because the phone ringing is loud and sudden","I agree, because the writer uses short sentences"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 lets you agree and disagree. Tom starts calm, then 'slid' shows that calm fading. Agreeing outright ignores the change; a loud phone and short sentences say nothing about Tom's feelings.","w":["","Smile spotted, but the question is the change: 'slid' shows the calm fading.","Phone is loud, but that's a sound, not Tom's feelings; it ignores 'slid'.","Short sentences affect pace, but they don't prove Tom is calm, and they miss 'slid'."]}
{"id":"gel-014","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":1,"q":"What kind of writing does Paper 1 Question 5 ask for?","o":["Descriptive or narrative writing, choosing one of two tasks","Writing a speech or letter that argues a viewpoint","Writing an article or review for a magazine","Writing a poem or a play script"],"a":0,"x":"Paper 1 Q5 is creative writing: you choose one task, such as a description or a story (from 2026 it may ask for a story opening). Arguing a viewpoint is Paper 2 Q5.","w":["","Mixed up with Paper 2 Q5, which is the viewpoint writing (speech, letter).","Articles belong to Paper 2 Q5 viewpoint writing; Paper 1 Q5 is description or story.","Poems and scripts are not set; Paper 1 Q5 is description or story."]}
{"id":"gel-015","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence contains a simile?","o":["The fog crept in like a patient thief.","The fog is a patient thief.","The fog stole the whole street.","Fog, fog and more fog."],"a":0,"x":"A simile compares using 'like' or 'as'. 'Is a thief' is a metaphor, 'stole the street' is personification without 'like' or 'as', and the last is repetition.","w":["","Metaphor: says the fog 'is' a thief, with no 'like' or 'as'.","Personification: fog does a human action, but no 'like' or 'as' comparison.","Repetition of 'fog' is a technique, but nothing is compared."]}
{"id":"gel-016","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which opening drops the reader straight into the action?","o":["My name is Sam and I am going to tell you about my life.","It was a Tuesday and I had cereal for breakfast.","The window shattered before I had even reached the door.","Let me begin by describing the history of my town."],"a":2,"x":"Starting in the middle of an event grabs attention. The other openings introduce background slowly.","w":["Slow introduction of yourself; nothing is happening yet.","Dull routine detail with no action or hook, so no drop into events.","","Opens with history, which delays the action rather than starting in it."]}
{"id":"gel-017","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence shows a feeling instead of just telling the reader?","o":["She was very nervous about the interview.","She felt anxious and worried all the time.","Nervous was how she felt that day.","Her fingers drummed the table and her eyes kept flicking to the clock."],"a":3,"x":"Showing uses actions and details the reader can picture. The other sentences simply name the feeling.","w":["Names the feeling ('nervous') rather than showing it through details.","Just states 'anxious and worried', which is telling, not showing.","Still just names the feeling, only reworded.",""]}
{"id":"gel-018","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":1,"q":"Paper 1 Q5 is worth 40 marks. How many are for technical accuracy (spelling, punctuation, vocabulary, sentence variety)?","o":["16","8","24","20"],"a":0,"x":"Q5 has 24 marks for content and organisation and 16 for technical accuracy.","w":["","Only a fifth of the marks; technical accuracy is worth more than 8.","That's the content and organisation mark, not technical accuracy.","Half of 40, but marks are split 24 and 16, not evenly."]}
{"id":"gel-019","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","o":["The storm was loud, we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud, so we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud we hid in the cellar.","The storm was loud, so, we hid in the cellar."],"a":1,"x":"A comma plus a joining word such as 'so' links two main clauses. A comma alone is a comma splice, no punctuation is a run-on, and 'so' needs no comma after it.","w":["Comma splice: a comma alone can't join two main clauses.","","Run-on sentence: two main clauses need a comma and a joining word.","Extra comma after 'so'; the joining word needs no comma after it."]}
{"id":"gel-020","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which is a complex sentence?","o":["Although the rain had stopped, the path stayed slippery.","The rain had stopped, but the path stayed slippery.","The path stayed slippery after the rain.","Slippery and silent."],"a":0,"x":"A complex sentence has a main clause plus a subordinate clause ('Although the rain had stopped'). The second is compound, the third simple and the last a minor sentence.","w":["","Compound: 'but' joins two equal main clauses, no subordinate clause.","Simple: one clause only, with no subordinate clause.","Minor sentence with no verb, so not a complex sentence."]}
{"id":"gel-021","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which version has every word and apostrophe correct?","o":["Their going to the beach. It's a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. It's a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. Its a long way, but they're sure it will be fun.","They're going to the beach. It's a long way, but there sure it will be fun."],"a":1,"x":"'They're' means they are, 'it's' means it is, and 'their' and 'there' are different words. Each wrong version has one slip.","w":["'Their' (belonging to them) is used where 'They're' (they are) is needed.","","'Its' without an apostrophe is possessive; 'it is' needs 'It's'.","'There' is a place; 'they're' (they are) is needed here."]}
{"id":"gel-022","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Question 1, what must you do?","o":["Choose the one false statement from a list of five","Write four sentences about the writer's language","Answer four multiple-choice questions on the opening lines","Choose the four true statements from a list of eight"],"a":3,"x":"Paper 2 Q1 (4 marks) gives eight statements about a short part of one source, and you pick the four that are true.","w":["Q1 asks for the four TRUE statements out of eight, not one false one out of five.","Q1 is not a writing task; you choose statements, not write sentences.","Not separate quiz questions: you shade four true statements out of eight.",""]}
{"id":"gel-023","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":2,"q":"Source: \"The council closed the library on 3 May. Residents, mostly pensioners, protested, though one teenager said she rarely used it.\" Which statement is true?","o":["The council closed the library in June.","The library was closed on 3 May.","The teenager used the library every day.","Only teenagers protested."],"a":1,"x":"True statements are supported by what the source says. The date 3 May is stated; the other options contradict the source.","w":["Wrong date: the source says 3 May, not June.","","Misread: she 'rarely used it', not every day.","Overgeneralised: residents, mostly pensioners, protested, not only teenagers."]}
{"id":"gel-024","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":1,"q":"What does Paper 2 Question 2 ask you to do with the two sources?","o":["Pick out facts from just one of the two sources","Analyse the writer's use of language in one of them","Infer differences (or similarities) between them, using both","Decide how far you agree with a statement about both"],"a":2,"x":"Q2 (8 marks) asks what you can infer about differences, or in some papers similarities, using evidence from both sources. Language in one source is Q3; Q4 compares perspectives.","w":["Q2 uses both sources and asks for inference, not just facts from one.","Language analysis of one source is Q3, not Q2.","","Agreeing with a statement is the Q4 style; Q2 asks for inferences about differences."]}
{"id":"gel-025","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":2,"q":"Source A: \"Gran loves the new bus route; it takes her to market in ten minutes.\" Source B: \"The new route is awful; it adds twenty minutes to my journey.\" Which sentence sums up a difference?","o":["Source A shows the route as slow, whereas Source B shows it as quick","Both sources show the route as quick and convenient","Source A shows the route as quick, whereas Source B shows it as slow","Source B mentions a market, whereas Source A does not"],"a":2,"x":"A strong Q2 line states each source's view, links them with 'whereas', and matches the evidence. A mentions the market, and only A says the route is quick.","w":["Swapped the sources: A calls the route quick, B says it adds time.","Ignores Source B, which says the route is awful and slow.","","Reversed and trivial: only A mentions the market, and it isn't the key difference."]}
{"id":"gel-026","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":3,"q":"Source A: \"Every evening, gangs of teenagers swarm over the park.\" Source B: \"We meet in the park because nowhere else is free, and we take our litter home.\" Which is the best inference?","o":["A shows teenagers as responsible; B shows them as a threat","A shows teenagers as a threat; B shows them as responsible, with few choices","Both sources present teenagers as damaging the park","A says the park is clean, whereas B says it is dirty"],"a":1,"x":"'Gangs' and 'swarm' suggest menace; B's litter and lack of alternatives suggest responsibility. Good Q2 answers infer from details like this.","w":["Swapped the sources: 'swarm' makes A the threat, B is the responsible one.","","Misses B, where they take litter home and so are not damaging the park.","Invents a clean/dirty contrast that neither source mentions."]}
{"id":"gel-027","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":1,"q":"How many marks is Paper 2 Question 3 worth?","o":["8","16","20","12"],"a":3,"x":"Paper 2 marks are Q1 4, Q2 8, Q3 12, Q4 16 and Q5 40.","w":["That is Q2, the 8-mark inference question.","That is Q4, the 16-mark comparison question.","20 is Paper 1 Q4; Paper 2 Q3 is worth 12.",""]}
{"id":"gel-028","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"Surely nobody wants to see our park turned into a car park?\"","o":["A statistic","A rhetorical question","Personification","Onomatopoeia"],"a":1,"x":"The writer asks a question that needs no answer, to make the reader agree. There is no number, no human quality given to an object and no sound word.","w":["No number or figure appears in the sentence.","","Nothing here is given human qualities; it is a question.","No sound word like 'bang' or 'hiss' appears."]}
{"id":"gel-029","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":2,"q":"Which technique is used in: \"The plan is wasteful, rushed and dangerous.\"","o":["A rhetorical question","Personification","A list of three","A simile"],"a":2,"x":"Three adjectives in a row make a list of three, which gives rhythm and builds the point. There is no question, human action or 'like/as' comparison.","w":["The sentence makes a statement, not a question.","No object is given human actions or feelings.","","No 'like' or 'as' comparison is made."]}
{"id":"gel-030","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":2,"q":"In \"Tourists invaded our quiet village\", what does \"invaded\" suggest?","o":["The tourists arrived politely in small numbers","The writer is warmly welcoming the tourists","The village is a very large and noisy city","The tourists are like a hostile army, showing the writer's resentment"],"a":3,"x":"'Invaded' has connotations of enemy attack, so it presents the tourists as unwelcome and the writer as angry.","w":["Missed the connotations: 'invaded' is aggressive, not polite.","Mixed up the tone: 'invaded' shows resentment, not a welcome.","The text calls it a quiet village, not a large noisy city.",""]}
{"id":"gel-031","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":1,"q":"Paper 2 Question 4 asks you to compare what?","o":["How the two writers present their viewpoints, and the methods they use","The facts that appear in both sources only","The spelling and punctuation of the two sources","How far you agree with each writer"],"a":0,"x":"Q4 (16 marks) compares the writers' perspectives on a shared topic, commenting on the methods they use, with evidence from both whole sources.","w":["","Q4 is about viewpoints and methods, not just shared facts.","Spelling and punctuation are not assessed in Q4.","That is a Paper 1 Q4 evaluation; Paper 2 Q4 compares the writers."]}
{"id":"gel-032","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":3,"q":"Which sentence is the best start to a Paper 2 Q4 comparison?","o":["Source A is about a plan. Source B is about a plan too.","Source A opposes the plan. Source B has a headline.","Whereas A calmly opposes the plan with facts, B angrily backs it with emotion.","Source A is good and Source B is bad."],"a":2,"x":"A good comparison links both writers' perspectives (against, for) in one sentence with a comparing word like 'whereas', and comments on their methods (facts, emotive language).","w":["Vague and descriptive: it only says both are about a plan, with no comparison.","Mentions a headline but never compares the writers' views or methods.","","Simple good/bad judgement with no evidence or methods."]}
{"id":"gel-033","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":3,"q":"A: \"Think of your child alone on a dark road at six in the morning.\" B: \"Official figures show only 2% of these journeys lead to any harm.\" Which comparison of methods is accurate?","o":["A uses a statistic to stir fear; B uses an emotive command to reassure","Both writers use statistics to stir fear","A uses a rhetorical question; B uses alliteration","A uses an emotive command to stir fear; B uses a statistic to reassure"],"a":3,"x":"A's imperative and imagery aim at emotion; B's figure aims at calm reassurance. Neither uses a question or alliteration. In Q4, comments like this support a comparison of perspectives.","w":["Swapped the methods: A's emotive command stirs fear; B's statistic reassures.","B's 2% figure is meant to reassure, and A uses no statistic.","Neither a rhetorical question nor alliteration appears in the quotes.",""]}
{"id":"gel-034","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which task is typical of Paper 2 Question 5?","o":["Write the opening of a story set in a forest","Write an article arguing a view on homework for a magazine","Analyse the structure of a poem","Summarise the differences between two sources"],"a":1,"x":"Paper 2 Q5 asks you to present a viewpoint in a form such as an article, letter or speech, for a stated audience.","w":["Story openings are Paper 1 Q5 (descriptive/narrative writing).","","Analysing a poem is not part of English Language Paper 2.","That is Paper 2 Q2, not the writing task in Q5."]}
{"id":"gel-035","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Which opening suits a formal letter urging a headteacher to change a rule?","o":["Hey Mrs P, listen up, this new rule is rubbish!","Once upon a time, a school had a rule.","Dear Mrs Patel, I am writing to urge you to reconsider the new lunch rule.","Chapter One: The Rule"],"a":2,"x":"Audience and purpose decide the register. A formal letter to a headteacher needs a polite greeting and a clear statement of purpose.","w":["Too casual and rude for a letter to a headteacher.","Fairy-tale opening, wrong form and purpose for a formal letter.","","A story-style chapter title suits fiction, not a formal letter."]}
{"id":"gel-036","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence best uses a counter-argument?","o":["Homework is bad. It is bad. Homework is bad.","Some say homework builds discipline; however, tired pupils learn far less.","Everyone knows I am right and nobody can disagree.","I think homework because I do."],"a":1,"x":"A counter-argument acknowledges another view and then answers it, which makes a persuasive case more convincing.","w":["Only repeats the same point, with no other view acknowledged or answered.","","Overconfident claim that ignores other views, so no counter-argument.","Weak and unclear: no opposing view is considered or answered."]}
{"id":"gel-037","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Q5 (40 marks), how many marks are for content and organisation?","o":["24","16","12","30"],"a":0,"x":"Content and organisation carry 24 marks; technical accuracy carries 16.","w":["","That is the technical accuracy mark; content and organisation gets the larger share.","Too low; content and organisation gets 24 of the 40, with 16 for accuracy.","Too high; with 16 for technical accuracy, only 24 of the 40 are left."]}
{"id":"gel-038","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?","o":["The road was empty; and the shops were dark.","Because the road was empty; the shops were dark.","The road; was empty and the shops were dark.","The road was empty; the shops were dark."],"a":3,"x":"A semicolon links two complete, closely related main clauses. It is not used with 'and' after it, after a subordinate clause or in the middle of a clause.","w":["A semicolon replaces a joining word; don't put 'and' straight after it.","A semicolon can't follow a subordinate clause starting 'Because'; a comma is needed.","It splits the subject from its verb; each side must be a full clause.",""]}
{"id":"gel-039","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":2,"q":"A speech refers to books belonging to several pupils. Which is correct?","o":["the pupil's books","the pupils' books","the pupils book's","the pupils's books"],"a":1,"x":"For a plural noun ending in s, the apostrophe goes after the s. 'Pupil's' would mean one pupil.","w":["Apostrophe before the s means one pupil, but the question says several.","","The apostrophe is put on 'book's', but the books don't own anything.","Adds an extra s; a plural ending in s only needs the apostrophe."]}
{"id":"gel-040","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":3,"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","o":["However the council refused, so the residents, who were furious marched.","However, the council refused so, the residents who were furious, marched.","However, the council refused, so the residents who, were furious, marched.","However, the council refused, so the residents, who were furious, marched."],"a":3,"x":"The opening 'However' needs a comma, a comma comes before 'so' joining the clauses, and the extra detail 'who were furious' needs a comma on both sides.","w":["Missing the comma after 'However' and the one closing 'who were furious'.","Comma after 'so' is wrong, and 'who were furious' lacks its first comma.","The first comma should go before 'who', not after it.",""]}
{"id":"gel-041","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":1,"q":"How is the Spoken Language endorsement in GCSE English Language assessed?","o":["As 20% of the GCSE grade, marked by an examiner","In Paper 1, as an extra written question","By your teacher, reported as Pass, Merit or Distinction, separate from the grade","Graded 9 to 1 like the written papers"],"a":2,"x":"Spoken Language is a teacher-assessed talk reported separately as Pass, Merit or Distinction (or Not classified). It does not count towards the 9-1 grade.","w":["It isn't worth 20% or examiner-marked; it's teacher-assessed and separate.","It isn't a written question; it is a spoken talk and is not part of Paper 1.","","It gets no 9 to 1 grade; it's reported as Pass, Merit or Distinction."]}
{"id":"gel-042","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":2,"q":"Which is the best way to plan a prepared talk?","o":["Write a script and read every word without looking up","Memorise a list of long words and drop them in anywhere","Choose a topic that interests you and plan a clear opening, middle and ending","Cram in as many facts as possible, whatever the topic"],"a":2,"x":"A prepared talk is judged on content, organisation and delivery for an audience. Clear structure and real interest in the topic help.","w":["Reading a script word for word loses eye contact and sounds flat.","Random long words don't make a clear talk; structure and meaning matter more.","","Too many facts with no interest or structure leaves the audience lost."]}
{"id":"gel-043","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":2,"q":"A classmate asks an unexpected question after your talk. What is the best response?","o":["Listen carefully, pause to think, then answer clearly with detail or an example","Say \"dunno\" and look at your notes","Repeat the start of your talk word for word","Answer a different question you had prepared"],"a":0,"x":"Responding to questions is assessed. Listening, taking a moment and giving a clear, detailed answer shows the skill.","w":["","'Dunno' shows no listening or detail, so you lose marks for responding.","Repeating your talk doesn't answer the question that was asked.","Dodging the question doesn't show you can respond to what was asked."]}
{"id":"gel-044","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence is in Standard English?","o":["We was not at the meeting.","We weren't never at the meeting.","Us were not at the meeting.","We were not at the meeting."],"a":3,"x":"Standard English uses 'we were' and avoids double negatives and 'us' as a subject.","w":["'We was' is a non-standard verb form; Standard English uses 'we were'.","'Weren't never' is a double negative, which is non-standard.","'Us' is an object pronoun, so it can't be the subject; use 'we'.",""]}
{"id":"gel-045","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":2,"q":"Which phrase suits the start of a formal talk?","o":["Right, so basically, like, this is a thing.","I would like to begin by explaining why this issue matters.","Oi, listen up, I'm gonna tell ya something.","Soz, I'm not ready but I'll try anyway."],"a":1,"x":"A formal setting calls for clear Standard English and a confident, polite tone, not slang or filler words.","w":["Filler words like 'basically' and 'like' sound unprepared, not formal.","","Slang like 'Oi' and 'gonna' is far too casual for a formal talk.","Apologising and saying you're not ready weakens your confidence and tone."]}
{"id":"gel-046","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":1,"q":"Extract: \"The lighthouse stood on a thin spit of rock. Every evening, old Tom climbed its ninety steps to light the lamp.\" Which statement is true?","o":["Tom climbs ninety steps each evening to light the lamp","The lighthouse stands on a large island","Tom has only just started work at the lighthouse","Tom climbs the steps each morning to light the lamp"],"a":0,"x":"The text states that Tom climbs ninety steps every evening to light the lamp. The trap is changing one detail, such as the time of day, or adding facts the text never gives.","w":["","The text says a thin spit of rock, not a large island; this changes the detail.","Nothing says he is new; \"every evening\" describes a regular routine, not a first day.","Swaps \"evening\" for \"morning\"; one changed detail makes the statement false."]}
{"id":"gel-047","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":2,"q":"Extract: \"By six o'clock the market was nearly empty. Sam counted his coins twice, then slipped them into his sock and set off home, whistling.\" Which statement is true?","o":["The market is completely empty at six o'clock","Sam is whistling because he has had a good day","Sam puts his coins into his sock after counting them","Sam puts his coins into his pocket and walks home"],"a":2,"x":"The text says Sam slipped his coins into his sock after counting them. The traps are \"nearly empty\" turned into \"completely empty\", and guessing why he whistles, which the text does not say.","w":["Turns \"nearly empty\" into \"completely empty\"; nearly empty means it is not completely empty yet.","The reason for whistling is a guess; the text states he whistles, not why.","","Replaces \"sock\" with \"pocket\"; a small changed detail makes it false."]}
{"id":"gel-048","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":1,"q":"Extract: \"The classroom smelled of chalk and wet coats. Snow pressed against the window. Ms Reid sat marking at her desk while twenty children stared at their books.\" Which statement is true?","o":["Ms Reid is angry with the class","Ms Reid is teaching from the board","The classroom smells of chalk and damp books","Twenty children are staring at their books"],"a":3,"x":"The text states that twenty children stared at their books. Anger is a guess, Ms Reid is marking not teaching, and the smell is chalk and wet coats, not damp books.","w":["Anger is not stated; this is a guess from the quiet scene.","She is marking at her desk, not teaching; this misreads what she is doing.","The smell is chalk and wet coats; \"damp books\" swaps in a detail from nowhere.",""]}
{"id":"gel-049","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q1","lv":3,"q":"Extract: \"Priya had promised to be home by nine. Now the clock above the shop door showed ten past nine, and she was still running, her bag banging against her hip, the harbour lights far behind her.\" Which is true?","o":["Priya promised to be home by ten past nine","Priya is still running when the clock shows ten past nine","Priya is running towards the harbour lights","Priya is running because someone is chasing her"],"a":1,"x":"The text says the clock shows ten past nine and she is still running. The promise was nine, not ten past. The harbour lights are behind her. A chaser is never mentioned, so that is a guess.","w":["Mixes up the promise (nine) with the time on the clock (ten past nine).","","The lights are \"far behind her\", so she is running away from them, not towards them.","No chaser is mentioned; this explains her running with an invented reason."]}
{"id":"gel-050","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":2,"q":"Read: \"Waves charged at the cliff. Spray exploded. The little boat was wounded.\" What do these word choices together suggest about the sea?","o":["A real battle is taking place on the shore","The sea is violent and dangerous, like a battle","The sea is calm, but the boat is old and weak","The writer is giving plain facts about the weather"],"a":1,"x":"'Charged', 'exploded' and 'wounded' come from the language of war. Together they make the sea seem violent and threatening. The trap is reading them literally instead of as a comparison.","w":["Takes the war words literally; they describe the sea figuratively, not a real fight.","","Ignores the force of 'charged' and 'exploded'; these words suggest violence, not calm.","Misses the effect; the vivid verbs are chosen to create danger, not to give plain facts."]}
{"id":"gel-051","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q2","lv":3,"q":"Read: \"She searched every room, every cupboard, every dusty corner of the silent house. Nothing.\" Which comment best analyses the effect of the last sentence?","o":["'Nothing' is a pronoun, which shows that the house is empty","The repeated 'every' is a simile showing that she is a careful person","It is a short sentence, so it is quick and easy for the reader to read","The abrupt one-word sentence feels final, stressing her disappointment"],"a":3,"x":"After a long, flowing list, the one-word sentence stops suddenly. This feels final and stresses her disappointment and the emptiness. Strong answers link the sentence form to an effect, not just name a word class.","w":["Only names a word class and does not explain any effect on the reader.","Wrong technique: 'every' is repeated, not compared using 'like' or 'as', so it is not a simile.","Vague and about reading speed; it does not explain the effect of the sudden short sentence.",""]}
{"id":"gel-052","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":2,"q":"A student writes: \"The writer uses the verb 'crept' to make the reader feel tense.\" Why would this get little credit in a Q3 answer?","o":["It comments on one word, which is language, not how the whole text is structured","It names an effect, but Q3 does not allow any effects to be named","It quotes from the text, which Q3 does not allow","It is correct, but it needs to be about the opening only"],"a":0,"x":"Q3 is about structure: how the extract is organised and where the focus shifts. Commenting on a single verb is language analysis, which belongs to Q2. The trap is mixing the two up.","w":["","Q3 asks for effects such as tension; naming an effect is expected, not banned.","Short quotations are welcome in Q3 as evidence; the problem is what is being analysed.","Q3 covers the whole extract, not just the opening, and the problem here is the focus on one word."]}
{"id":"gel-053","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q3","lv":3,"q":"An extract begins inside a noisy, busy market. Then it narrows to a boy staring at a locked box. It ends with him hearing his name whispered. Which comment best explains the tension?","o":["Focus narrows from the crowd to one boy and box, then a whisper leaves a mystery","A flashback in the middle slows the story and releases the tension","The focus stays on the busy crowd throughout, so the tension comes from noise","Focus widens from the boy to the whole market, so the reader feels safe at the end"],"a":0,"x":"The focus moves from a wide, noisy crowd to a tight focus on the boy and box. The whispered name at the end leaves a mystery unresolved, building tension. The trap is describing a shift that is not there.","w":["","No flashback is described; the extract moves through focus, not back in time.","The focus does not stay on the crowd; it narrows to the boy and the box.","Reversed: the focus narrows rather than widens, and the whispered ending is unsettling, not safe."]}
{"id":"gel-054","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":1,"q":"Paper 1 Question 4 says: 'Focus this part of your answer on the second half of the source.' What should you do?","o":["Use evidence from the whole extract equally, including the opening","Use evidence only from the part of the source the question names","Use evidence from the first paragraph, then compare it with the ending","Use ideas about the writer's other books to back up your view"],"a":1,"x":"Q4 names the part of the extract to focus on, and your evidence must come from that part. The trap is quoting from the opening or from outside the extract, which loses the focus.","w":["Q4 points you to one named part, so spreading evidence across the whole extract loses focus.","","The named part is later in the extract, so the first paragraph is the wrong place to look.","Q4 only tests the extract on the page, so the writer's other books are not evidence."]}
{"id":"gel-055","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":2,"q":"Extract: 'The corridor stretched ahead, dark and silent. Her footsteps echoed, each one louder than the last.' Statement: 'The setting feels threatening.' Which comment is best?","o":["I agree: 'The corridor stretched ahead, dark and silent. Her footsteps echoed.'","The writer uses adjectives and a long sentence to describe the corridor.","I think corridors are scary, so I agree that the setting feels threatening.","Agree: 'dark and silent' feels menacing; 'louder than the last' builds fear"],"a":3,"x":"The best answer gives a clear judgement, short quotations and a comment on their effect. The traps are copying out whole sentences, naming techniques without effect, or giving personal opinion.","w":["It copies out long quotations with no comment on their effect.","It names a method but says nothing about its effect or whether the statement is right.","Personal opinion about corridors gives no evidence from the extract.",""]}
{"id":"gel-056","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q4","lv":3,"q":"Extract: 'Dad packed the car at dawn. I counted the lamp-posts and said nothing. My stomach felt like a stone.' Statement: 'The narrator is excited.' Which response is best?","o":["Agree: 'Dad packed the car at dawn' shows the family is keen to leave","Agree: 'counted the lamp-posts' shows they cannot wait to arrive","Disagree: 'like a stone' suggests dread; 'said nothing' shows withdrawal","Disagree: 'at dawn' shows the narrator is angry about the early start"],"a":2,"x":"Judge the narrator's feelings, not Dad's actions. The simile 'felt like a stone' suggests dread and 'said nothing' suggests withdrawal. Both go against 'excited'. The other options misread or invent evidence.","w":["This is Dad's action, not the narrator's feelings, and it ignores the stone simile.","Counting could mean many things, and it ignores the dread in 'stone' and the silence.","","'At dawn' only gives the time; nothing in the extract shows anger, so this is unsupported."]}
{"id":"gel-057","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":1,"q":"AQA Paper 1 Question 5 is worth 40 marks. How are these marks shared?","o":["16 for content and organisation, 24 for technical accuracy","24 for content and organisation, 16 for technical accuracy","20 for content and organisation, 20 for technical accuracy","30 for content and organisation, 10 for technical accuracy"],"a":1,"x":"Most marks (24) reward ideas, structure and language choices. The other 16 reward spelling, punctuation and sentence control. Do not think accuracy matters more than ideas, or that the marks are split evenly.","w":["Reverses the split: technical accuracy is worth fewer marks than content and organisation.","","Not an even split: content and organisation carries more marks than technical accuracy.","Gives accuracy too few marks; 16 marks are for spelling, punctuation and sentence control."]}
{"id":"gel-058","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5a","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence from a story is punctuated correctly?","o":["The rain stopped, the street fell silent.","The rain stopped and, the street fell silent.","The rain stopped the street, fell silent.","The rain stopped; the street fell silent."],"a":3,"x":"A semicolon can join two closely linked complete sentences. A comma alone cannot, because that makes a comma splice. Misplaced commas are another common technical accuracy error.","w":["Comma splice: a comma alone cannot join two complete sentences.","Misplaced comma: it wrongly cuts 'and' off from the clause it introduces.","Misplaced comma: it separates 'the street' from its verb 'fell', so the sentence makes no sense.",""]}
{"id":"gel-059","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":2,"q":"Which sentence punctuates direct speech correctly?","o":["\"Don't move\", whispered Mia, \"or he'll hear us.\"","\"Don't move,\" Whispered Mia, \"or he'll hear us.\"","\"Don't move,\" whispered Mia, \"or he'll hear us.\"","\"Don't move,\" whispered Mia. \"or he'll hear us.\""],"a":2,"x":"The comma goes inside the speech marks. The speech tag 'whispered Mia' stays lower case because the sentence carries on. The second part of the speech continues the same sentence, so it has no capital letter.","w":["The comma must go inside the speech marks, before the closing quotation mark, not after it.","The speech tag 'whispered' needs a lower-case w, because the sentence has not ended.","","A full stop ends the sentence too early, yet the next part then starts with a small letter."]}
{"id":"gel-060","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p1-q5b","lv":1,"q":"Which sentence uses a semicolon correctly?","o":["When the lake froze; we skated until dark.","The lake was frozen; and we skated until dark.","We skated on; the frozen lake until dark.","The lake was frozen; we skated until dark."],"a":3,"x":"A semicolon joins two complete, closely linked main clauses. It does not go after a subordinate clause, beside 'and', or in the middle of a clause.","w":["'When the lake froze' is a subordinate clause that cannot stand alone, so it needs a comma, not a semicolon.","A semicolon already does the joining job of 'and', so using both together is a common slip.","The semicolon splits one clause in the middle, leaving 'the frozen lake until dark', which is not a full sentence.",""]}
{"id":"gel-061","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Question 1, what should you use to decide whether a statement is true?","o":["What you already know about the topic from elsewhere","What the writer probably meant but did not say","What the lines named in the question actually say","What most people would agree is sensible"],"a":2,"x":"Q1 only rewards what the named lines say. Outside knowledge, guesses and common sense can lead you to choose a statement the lines do not support.","w":["Outside knowledge is a trap: a statement can be true in real life but not supported by the lines.","Guessing beyond the lines is risky; a true statement must be clearly supported by the lines given.","","'Sensible' is not evidence. The statement must be backed by the lines given."]}
{"id":"gel-062","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":2,"q":"Source: \"Maya cycles four miles to school. The ride takes 25 minutes, but the bus would take 40 because of traffic. She says cycling keeps her fit.\" Which statement is true?","o":["Maya's bike journey takes less time than the bus would.","The bus journey to Maya's school takes 25 minutes.","Maya cycles mainly because she wants to save money.","Maya would rather catch the bus than cycle."],"a":0,"x":"The ride takes 25 minutes and the bus 40, so the bike is quicker. The trap is swapping the times round or adding reasons the source never gives.","w":["","The times are swapped: 25 minutes is the bike ride, and the bus would take 40.","Money is never mentioned; her stated reason is fitness, so this reason is invented.","The source says she cycles and values the fitness. It never says she prefers the bus."]}
{"id":"gel-063","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":2,"q":"Source: \"After the match, the manager said the team had played badly. Fans left early, and the stadium was half empty by the final whistle.\" Which statement is true?","o":["The fans were angry with the manager.","The stadium was empty before the final whistle.","The team lost the match.","The manager said the team had played badly."],"a":3,"x":"This is stated directly. The other options are inferences or exaggerations: the source gives no result, no fan feelings, and says 'half empty', not empty.","w":["This is an inference. Fans leaving early does not say why, or that they blamed the manager.","This exaggerates: the stadium was 'half empty', not empty.","The result is never stated. Playing badly does not prove the team lost.",""]}
{"id":"gel-064","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q1","lv":3,"q":"Source: \"In 1998 the village shop had six staff. Today it has two and opens only in the mornings. Mr Patel, the owner, says he will keep it open as long as he can.\" Which pair is BOTH true?","o":["The shop now has four fewer staff; Mr Patel plans to close it.","It had six staff in 1998; it now opens only in the mornings.","Mr Patel opened the shop in 1998; it now has two staff.","It now opens only in the afternoons; it has two staff."],"a":1,"x":"Both parts are stated: six staff in 1998 and morning-only opening now. A statement with one true half and one false half is false, so check every part.","w":["The first half is true (6 − 2 = 4), but Mr Patel says he will keep it open, not close it.","","Nobody says Mr Patel opened the shop in 1998. Only the staff numbers are given.","The shop opens only in the mornings, so the first half is false even though two staff is right."]}
{"id":"gel-065","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":1,"q":"In Paper 2 Question 2, which is the best way to use evidence from the sources?","o":["Copy out a long section from each source so that nothing is missed","Use short quotations from both sources and explain what each suggests","Quote only from the source that shows the clearest difference","Name the language technique in each quotation and explain its effect"],"a":1,"x":"Q2 rewards short, well-chosen quotations from both sources, with an inference about what each suggests. Long copying shows no understanding. Technique analysis belongs to Q3, not Q2.","w":["Long copied chunks waste time and show no inference; short quotations are better.","","Q2 needs evidence from both sources, so quoting only one source loses marks.","Naming techniques and effects is Q3 language analysis; Q2 is about inferring differences."]}
{"id":"gel-066","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":2,"q":"Source A: \"Mr Patel says the gym 'transformed' his week; he now goes at six every morning.\" Source B: \"I joined in January and have been twice. The fee just sits in my bank.\" Which is the best difference?","o":["A shows someone who joined the gym, whereas B shows someone who never joined","A shows someone who goes early, whereas B shows someone who goes late at night","A shows the gym as too expensive, whereas B shows it as good value","A shows a keen, regular user, whereas B rarely goes and wastes the fee"],"a":3,"x":"The best answer infers a clear contrast from both sources. 'Transformed' and 'every morning' suggest commitment. 'Twice' and 'just sits' suggest B's money is wasted. Beware inventing details the sources do not give.","w":["B says 'I joined in January', so this misreads B as never joining.","Invented detail: B never says when they go, only that they have been twice.","Swaps and invents: only B mentions the fee, and it is seen as wasted, not good value.",""]}
{"id":"gel-067","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q2","lv":3,"q":"Q2 asks for differences in how the writers feel about city life. Source A: \"The city hums; I never feel alone.\" Source B: \"The noise follows me into every room, and I have never felt so alone.\" Best answer?","o":["A finds the city lively and comforting, whereas B finds it intrusive and isolating","Both writers find the city noisy, so it affects them in the same way","A finds the city peaceful, whereas B finds it too noisy to live in","A feels lonely in the city, whereas B feels surrounded by company"],"a":0,"x":"The question asks for differences. 'Hums' and 'never feel alone' suggest comfort; 'follows me into every room' and 'so alone' suggest intrusion and isolation. Spotting shared noise is a similarity, not the difference asked for.","w":["","This gives a similarity, but the question asks for differences; their reactions to the noise are opposite.","'Hums' suggests busy, comforting life, not peace, and B's main problem is loneliness, not just noise.","Swaps the sources: A says 'never feel alone' and B says 'never felt so alone'."]}
{"id":"gel-068","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":2,"q":"A writer describes a closure: \"The mine closed. Nobody spoke.\" What is the best comment on the effect of these short sentences?","o":["They sound blunt and final, which shows the shock and silence after the closure","They sound relaxed and cheerful, which shows the writer is pleased","They make a list of three, which builds the point step by step","They ask a rhetorical question, which makes the reader agree"],"a":0,"x":"Short, blunt sentences feel stark and final. They stress the shock of the closure and the silence after it. The trap is naming a technique that is not there, such as a list of three.","w":["","Misreads the tone: blunt sentences about a closure suggest shock, not cheerfulness.","There are only two short statements, not three items, so this is not a list of three.","Both sentences are statements; neither is a question, so no rhetorical question is used."]}
{"id":"gel-069","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q3","lv":3,"q":"A writer says: \"The council ripped out the old library.\" Which comment is the strongest language analysis for Q3?","o":["The writer uses a verb to make the sentence more interesting for the reader","The writer says the council removed the library, which tells us what happened","'Ripped out' is a violent verb, suggesting the council acted harshly and carelessly","The sentence starts the paragraph, so it grabs the reader's attention"],"a":2,"x":"Strong analysis picks a precise word, explains its connotations (violence, carelessness) and links them to the writer's view. The trap is vague comments, like 'grabs attention', or simply retelling the sentence.","w":["Too vague: it names no word, no connotation and no specific effect on the reader.","This only retells the sentence in other words and does not explain the effect of the language.","","Generic: 'grabs attention' fits any sentence, and position in a paragraph is structure, not the effect of word choices."]}
{"id":"gel-070","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":1,"q":"Which pair of linking words is most useful for showing a difference between the two writers in a Q4 answer?","o":["First of all / Finally","Whereas / In contrast","Because / Therefore","Also / Then"],"a":1,"x":"Comparing words like 'whereas' and 'in contrast' link the two writers' viewpoints in one sentence. Sequence words and cause words organise steps or reasons. They do not compare the sources.","w":["These show order, not difference, and tend to lead to describing Source A, then Source B, separately.","","These show cause and effect, so they link reasons rather than contrasting the two writers' viewpoints.","These add or order points, so they do not signal a difference between the writers."]}
{"id":"gel-071","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":2,"q":"A: \"Tourists treat our village like a theme park, and we are the exhibits.\" B: \"Visitors bring jobs, and jobs bring hope.\" Which comparison of methods is accurate?","o":["A uses a simile and metaphor to show resentment; B repeats 'jobs' to build hope","A uses a statistic to show anger; B uses a simile to show hope","A uses a rhetorical question to show resentment; B uses a list to show hope","A uses repetition to show resentment; B uses a simile to show hope"],"a":0,"x":"A compares the village to a theme park (simile) and calls residents 'exhibits' (metaphor), showing resentment. B repeats 'jobs' to link jobs to hope. Check each method really appears in the quote before naming it.","w":["","A contains no statistic and B contains no simile, so these methods are named without being in the text.","A asks no question and B is not a list, so the methods are named without checking the quotes.","This swaps the methods: B uses repetition, A uses a simile and metaphor, and B has no simile."]}
{"id":"gel-072","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q4","lv":3,"q":"A: \"Our high street is dying, and the new car park will finish it off.\" B: \"The new car park will give shoppers a reason to come back.\" Which is the strongest Q4-style point?","o":["Both writers talk about the new car park and the high street.","A is negative and B is positive, so B is the better writer.","A's \"finish it off\" casts the car park as killer; B's \"come back\" casts it as cure","A uses a metaphor, \"dying\", and B uses a simile to sound hopeful."],"a":2,"x":"The strongest point compares both perspectives, hostile and hopeful, and links each to a quoted phrase and its effect. Just naming the topic or judging quality does not compare viewpoints. B uses no simile.","w":["This only describes the shared topic and does not compare the writers' viewpoints or methods.","Q4 does not ask which writer is better, and this gives no evidence or methods.","","'Dying' is a metaphor, but B contains no simile, so a method is named that is not there."]}
{"id":"gel-073","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":1,"q":"Paper 2 Question 5 is worth 40 marks. 16 of them are for technical accuracy (AO6). What does this reward?","o":["Using as many rhetorical devices as you can in every paragraph","Accurate spelling, punctuation and grammar, with varied vocabulary and sentences","Neat handwriting and filling as many pages as you possibly can","Agreeing with the opinion the examiner is most likely to hold"],"a":1,"x":"The 16 AO6 marks reward accurate spelling, punctuation and grammar, plus varied vocabulary and sentence structures. The other 24 marks are for content and organisation. Your opinion is never marked.","w":["Devices can help the 24 content marks, but technical accuracy is about correct, varied language.","","Handwriting is not marked, and length alone earns no marks.","Examiners mark how well you argue, not which side you take."]}
{"id":"gel-074","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":2,"q":"A student is writing a speech about phone use. Which sentence uses BOTH direct address and a rhetorical question?","o":["Teenagers spend around three hours online each day.","Phones are distracting, harmful and pointless.","Some people want phones banned, but others disagree.","You deserve better, so why are we still putting up with it?"],"a":3,"x":"\"You\" is direct address, and the question needs no answer, so it is rhetorical. A statistic, a list of three and a balanced view are different techniques. Spot each device by its features.","w":["This is a statistic (fact and figure). It has no direct address and no question.","This is a rule of three (a list of three). It has no direct address and no question.","This is a balanced view or counter-argument. It has neither direct address nor a question.",""]}
{"id":"gel-075","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5a","lv":3,"q":"An article argues that school should start later. Which conclusion is most effective?","o":["Later starts mean alert pupils, safer journeys and calmer mornings. Act now.","In conclusion, I have said school should start later. Thanks for reading.","Some may disagree, and they might be right, so perhaps nothing should change.","Another reason is that teenagers are tired. Also, buses are often late."],"a":0,"x":"A strong conclusion pulls the argument together, uses a memorable device such as a rule of three, and ends with a firm call to action. Weak endings only repeat the topic, doubt the view or add new points.","w":["","This only announces and repeats the topic. It is flat and does not leave the reader convinced.","This undermines the writer's own viewpoint at the end. Persuasion needs a confident close.","This adds new points instead of concluding, so the ending trails off."]}
{"id":"gel-076","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":2,"q":"You are writing an article. Which sentence avoids a comma splice and is punctuated correctly?","o":["Phones distract pupils, they should be banned in lessons.","Phones distract pupils, so they should be banned in lessons.","Phones distract pupils so, they should be banned in lessons.","Phones distract pupils, therefore they should be banned in lessons."],"a":1,"x":"A comma alone cannot join two full sentences; that is a comma splice. A comma plus a conjunction such as 'so' fixes it. Words like 'therefore' and 'however' are not conjunctions.","w":["This comma splice joins two full clauses with only a comma, so it needs a conjunction, full stop or semicolon.","","The comma is misplaced after 'so' when it belongs before it.","'Therefore' is not a conjunction, so this is still a comma splice and needs a semicolon or full stop."]}
{"id":"gel-077","s":"english_lang","t":"el-p2-q5b","lv":1,"q":"In a speech, which sentence uses every word correctly?","o":["Their is no excuse for dropping litter.","Young people should be proud of there town.","The new law will effect every driver.","They're ignoring a problem that affects us all."],"a":3,"x":"'They're' means 'they are', and 'affects' is the verb meaning 'has an impact on'. Mixing up homophones is a common spelling slip that loses technical accuracy marks.","w":["This homophone slip uses 'their', which shows ownership, where 'there is' is needed.","This homophone slip uses 'there', meaning a place, where 'their' is needed to show ownership.","This confuses affect and effect: the verb meaning 'have an impact on' is 'affect'.",""]}
{"id":"gel-078","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":1,"q":"A student is giving a formal talk to the class and teacher. Which sentence uses Standard English?","o":["We was surprised by how many students voted.","We were well surprised by how many students voted, like.","We never expected them students to vote.","We were surprised by how many students voted."],"a":3,"x":"Standard English uses correct grammar, such as 'we were', and avoids slang and dialect forms. The Spoken Language endorsement expects it in formal talks. It is about grammar and vocabulary, not accent.","w":["'We was' is non-standard subject-verb agreement. It should be 'we were'.","'Well' and 'like' are casual slang fillers, so this is too informal for a formal talk.","'Them students' is a non-standard form. It should be 'those students'.",""]}
{"id":"gel-079","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":2,"q":"After your talk, a classmate asks a question you cannot fully answer. Which reply best shows the skill of responding to questions?","o":["\"Good question. I don't know the exact figure, but I found that most students...\"","\"I don't know.\" Then look away and wait for the next question.","Invent a number that sounds convincing so you seem sure.","\"That wasn't in my talk, so I can't answer it.\""],"a":0,"x":"Responding well means listening, being honest about what you don't know, and answering with what you do know. Inventing facts or refusing to engage loses marks in 'responding to questions and feedback'.","w":["","A blunt 'I don't know' gives the audience nothing and shows no effort to respond.","Making up a number is dishonest and will weaken your credibility if it is challenged.","Refusing a question that links to your topic shows you are not listening or engaging."]}
{"id":"gel-080","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":2,"q":"A student's talk on school uniform has three main points. Which opening best helps the audience follow it?","o":["\"Uniform. Rules. Cost. Right, let's start.\"","\"I will make three points: cost, comfort and fairness. First, cost.\"","\"I have a lot to say, so I will say it as it comes into my head.\"","\"Before I start, I should say I did not have much time to prepare.\""],"a":1,"x":"Signposting tells listeners the plan, so they can follow the structure. Naming the three points and then starting the first one gives a clear route through the talk. Rambling or apologising does not.","w":["Single words with no explanation do not tell the audience what the talk will cover.","","Speaking 'as it comes' means no structure, so listeners are likely to get lost.","Apologising for poor preparation weakens the opening and gives no plan for the talk."]}
{"id":"gel-081","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl1","lv":3,"q":"A student is ending a formal talk that argues for less plastic waste. Which ending is the best match for a formal, persuasive purpose?","o":["\"So yeah, that's basically it, thanks for listening, I guess.\"","\"Plastic is bad. Plastic is really bad. It's bad, innit.\"","\"Plastic harms our seas, our wildlife and our future, so we must act now.\"","\"Many people have studied plastic in the sea over several years.\""],"a":2,"x":"A strong formal ending uses Standard English, a rule of three ('seas, wildlife and future') and a clear call to action. Slang and weak repetition lower the register, and a bare statement does not persuade.","w":["'So yeah' and 'I guess' are casual and uncertain, so the talk ends weakly with no call to action.","'Innit' is slang, and the repeated 'bad' is flat. It has no developed point or formal register.","","The register is formal, but it only states a fact. It does not persuade or call the audience to act."]}
{"id":"gel-082","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":1,"q":"In AQA GCSE English Language, how is your Spoken Language work, including answering questions, reported?","o":["As 20% of your overall 9–1 English Language grade","As a mark added to your Paper 2 total","As a separate Pass, Merit or Distinction, not part of the 9–1 grade","As its own 9–1 grade on your certificate"],"a":2,"x":"Your teacher assesses Spoken Language. It is reported separately as Pass, Merit, Distinction or Not Classified. The trap is thinking it adds marks to the 9–1 grade.","w":["It does not count towards the 9–1 grade at all, so it carries no percentage weighting.","It is not added to any exam paper because your teacher assesses it separately from Papers 1 and 2.","","It gets Pass, Merit or Distinction rather than a 9–1 number, so this confuses the two grading systems."]}
{"id":"gel-083","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":2,"q":"After your talk, a classmate asks a long question in two parts. You only caught the first part. What is the best thing to do?","o":["Answer the first part well, then politely ask them to repeat the second part","Guess what the second part was and answer that as well","Say you did not understand and give no answer at all","Answer only the first part and hope nobody notices"],"a":0,"x":"Responding well means listening and answering what was actually asked. Politely asking for a repeat shows good listening. The trap is guessing, which risks answering the wrong question.","w":["","Guessing may mean answering a question nobody asked, which shows you have not listened properly.","Giving up wastes the chance to give a clear, detailed answer to the part you did hear.","Ignoring half the question means you have not responded fully or shown careful listening."]}
{"id":"gel-084","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":2,"q":"Your teacher says: \"Good point about screen time, but you gave no evidence.\" Which reply best shows you can respond to feedback?","o":["\"My talk was fine. Evidence isn't needed for an opinion.\"","\"Sorry, I know my talk was bad.\" Then say nothing more.","Nod and say nothing, so you don't make a mistake.","\"Thanks. A survey linked late phone use to less sleep in teens.\""],"a":3,"x":"Good responses accept feedback and use it straight away, here by adding evidence. Arguing or going quiet does not show you can respond. The trap is treating feedback as an attack.","w":["This defensively rejects the feedback instead of taking it on board and improving the point.","Apologising without adding anything gives no detail and does not improve on the point made.","Staying silent gives no response at all, when responding means speaking clearly and in detail.",""]}
{"id":"gel-085","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":2,"q":"Your teacher asks: \"Why did you choose this topic?\" Which answer is in Standard English and gives a reason?","o":["\"Me and my mates was always playing it, so I picked it.\"","\"I chose it because my friends and I often play it.\"","\"I just picked it cos it's well good and that.\"","\"We was playing it loads, so it seemed the right one.\""],"a":1,"x":"Standard English is assessed in Spoken Language, including in answers. \"My friends and I often play it\" is correct grammar and gives a reason. The trap is \"me and my mates was\" or \"we was\", which are non-standard.","w":["\"Me and my mates was\" uses the wrong pronoun and verb agreement, so it is non-standard.","","\"Cos\", \"well good\" and \"and that\" are informal and vague, giving no clear reason.","\"We was\" should be \"we were\", so this non-standard verb agreement breaks Standard English."]}
{"id":"gel-086","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl2","lv":3,"q":"Asked \"Why should school start later, and would it suit every pupil?\", which reply best shows careful listening and a detailed answer?","o":["A long, detailed reason about teenage sleep, with nothing on the second part","\"Yes, it would suit everyone.\" Then stop.","Gives a reason with an example, then says pupils with long bus journeys might struggle","\"As I said in my talk.\" Then repeat the opening sentence."],"a":2,"x":"The question has two parts. The best reply answers both with detail and a fair point about limits. The trap is answering only one part, or repeating the talk.","w":["The detail is good, but ignoring the second part means the question is not fully answered.","This is short with no reasoning, and it skips the first part about why school should start later.","","Repeating the talk does not answer what was asked, showing the question was not listened to."]}
{"id":"gel-087","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":1,"q":"A student has a strong regional accent and is giving a formal talk. What should they do to speak Standard English?","o":["Change their accent to sound like a newsreader","Keep their accent but use Standard English grammar and vocabulary","Use longer, harder words than normal","Speak very fast so mistakes are less noticeable"],"a":1,"x":"Standard English is about grammar and vocabulary, not accent. You can keep your accent. The trap is thinking you must hide it.","w":["Standard English is not an accent, so there is no need to change how you sound.","","Long words don't make speech Standard English; the grammar must be correct and clear.","Speaking fast makes you harder to hear and understand; a formal talk needs a clear pace."]}
{"id":"gel-088","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":2,"q":"A student says: \"I seen the results and them figures was surprising.\" Which version is fully in Standard English?","o":["I seen the results and those figures were surprising.","I saw the results and them figures was surprising.","I have saw the results and those figures were surprising.","I saw the results and those figures were surprising."],"a":3,"x":"Two errors need fixing: 'seen' becomes 'saw' (or 'have seen'), and 'them figures was' becomes 'those figures were'. Fixing only one error is the trap.","w":["It fixes 'them' and 'was' but keeps 'I seen', which is a non-standard past tense.","It fixes 'seen' but keeps 'them figures was', which is still non-standard.","'Have saw' is wrong; it should be 'have seen' or simply 'saw'.",""]}
{"id":"gel-089","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":2,"q":"After your talk on school uniform, a classmate asks a question you did not expect. Which reply best uses formal Standard English and answers the question?","o":["Good question. I would argue that uniform reduces pressure, because everyone looks the same.","Er, dunno really, it's like, whatever, I s'pose.","Me and Sam reckon it ain't fair, to be fair.","That's not in my notes, so I can't say anything."],"a":0,"x":"Responding to questions is part of the assessment. A good reply is polite, clear and in Standard English, with a reason. The traps are slang, fillers or refusing to answer.","w":["","Fillers like 'er' and 'like' and slang like 'dunno' sound unsure and casual.","'Me and Sam reckon' and 'ain't' are non-standard; 'Sam and I' would be correct.","It is Standard English, but it refuses to answer the question at all."]}
{"id":"gel-090","s":"english_lang","t":"el-sl3","lv":3,"q":"You are speaking to a panel of councillors. Which sentence has Standard English grammar but is still too casual for a formal talk?","o":["Me and my team was working on the plan.","Young people haven't got no places to meet.","Loads of kids have nowhere to chill, which is pretty rubbish.","Many young people have nowhere to meet."],"a":2,"x":"Its grammar is Standard, but 'loads of kids', 'chill' and 'rubbish' are too informal. Standard English and formal register are different things, so check both.","w":["'Me' is used as a subject and 'was' follows a plural subject, so the grammar is non-standard.","'Haven't got no' is a double negative, so the grammar is non-standard.","","This is Standard English with suitably formal word choice, so it is not too casual."]}
